    {
      "path": "app/src/main/java/cloud/samlo/couch/update/Release.kt",
      "content": "`JSONObject(text)` parses the stored text a second time, right after `VersionJson.read(text)` has parsed it. It can't throw today because `read` already accepted the text. Still, `deserialize` now relies on `read` never becoming more lenient than `JSONObject` (any `JSONException` here would escape, even though the doc says broken text gives null). Also, the Int/Long `when` is a copy of the one in `VersionJson`/`SilentBlock`. Suggestion: parse once, then pass the `JSONObject` to a `read(json)` overload, or pull out a shared `wholeLong(raw: Any?): Long?` helper. Alternatively, wrap this in `runCatching` so the never-throws promise holds.",
      "suggestion_code": "val afterBox = runCatching { JSONObject(text).opt(AFTER_BOX) }.getOrNull().let { raw -\u003e\n    when (raw) {\n        is Int -\u003e raw.toLong()\n        is Long -\u003e raw\n        else -\u003e null\n    }\n}",
      "existing_code": "            val afterBox = when (val raw = JSONObject(text).opt(AFTER_BOX)) {",
      "start_line": 48,
      "end_line": 48,
      "category": "maintainability",
      "severity": "low"
    },
    {
      "path": "release.sh",
      "content": "`kevbox-home-latest.apk` is repointed before the host SHA-256 is compared with `local_sha`. If they don't match, the script dies with \"version.json was not written\", but the stable `latest` link already points at the bytes that failed the check. The link is moved too early in two more cases:\n- the `version.json` write fails afterwards;\n- `cleanup` deletes the tag.\n\nIn all three cases, anything that provisions from `latest` (see docs/kevbox/releasing.md) gets an APK that was never verified or published. Fix: run only install and `sha256sum` in this ssh call. Move the `ln -sfn` into a separate step after the SHA check, ideally in the same ssh call that moves `version.json` into place.",
      "suggestion_code": "host_sha=\"$(ssh -p \"$ssh_port\" \"$ssh_alias\" \"set -e; sudo install -D -m 644 /tmp/$remote_apk $remote_root/$remote_apk; rm -f /tmp/$remote_apk; cd $remote_root \u0026\u0026 sha256sum $remote_apk\" \\\n  | awk -v f=\"$remote_apk\" '$2 == f { print $1 }')\" || die \"...\"\n# ... after the SHA check, and together with the version.json mv:\n#   sudo ln -sfn $remote_apk $remote_root/kevbox-home-latest.apk",
      "existing_code": "host_sha=\"$(ssh -p \"$ssh_port\" \"$ssh_alias\" \"set -e; sudo install -D -m 644 /tmp/$remote_apk $remote_root/$remote_apk; rm -f /tmp/$remote_apk; sudo ln -sfn $remote_apk $remote_root/kevbox-home-latest.apk; cd $remote_root \u0026\u0026 sha256sum $remote_apk\" \\",
      "start_line": 215,
      "end_line": 215,
      "category": "bug",
      "severity": "medium"
    },
    {
      "path": "app/src/main/java/cloud/samlo/couch/data/Fetcher.kt",
      "content": "`Date` is treated as \"server now\", but that only holds for an uncached response. If tv.kevbox.dev sits behind a CDN or proxy cache, a cached `version.json` keeps the origin's `Date` and adds an `Age` header. The `Date` can then be minutes or hours in the past. `SilentRules.afterOnBox` turns that staleness into a positive gap, so `silentAfterBoxMs` lands later than intended. That delays the silent install and also shifts the 72 h safety-net window. Add the `Age` header (in seconds) to the `Date` value when it is present. Alternatively, send `Cache-Control: no-cache` on this request.",
      "existing_code": "                val date = try {\n                    connection.getHeaderFieldDate(\"Date\", 0L)\n                } catch (e: Exception) {\n                    0L\n                }",
      "start_line": 33,
      "end_line": 37,
      "category": "bug",
      "severity": "medium"
    },
    {
      "path": "app/src/main/java/cloud/samlo/couch/kevbox/update/SilentState.kt",
      "content": "`Snapshot` only holds data, so making it a `data class` would give it value `equals` and a readable `toString`. That helps JVM tests that compare `SilentState.of(...)` results and makes log output clearer.",
      "suggestion_code": "    data class Snapshot(val attempts: Int, val refused: Boolean)",
      "existing_code": "    class Snapshot(val attempts: Int, val refused: Boolean)",
      "start_line": 16,
      "end_line": 16,
      "category": "maintainability",
      "severity": "low"
    },
    {
      "path": "app/src/main/java/cloud/samlo/couch/kevbox/update/SilentInstall.kt",
      "content": "Mixing `+` concatenation with a template here is hard to read. Using a single template would be clearer.",
      "suggestion_code": "        Log.i(TAG, line(\"result\", code, result.name, \"status $status${message?.let { \" $it\" }.orEmpty()}\"))",
      "existing_code": "        Log.i(TAG, line(\"result\", code, result.name, \"status $status\" + (message?.let { \" $it\" } ?: \"\")))",
      "start_line": 232,
      "end_line": 232,
      "category": "style",
      "severity": "low"
    },
    {
      "path": "app/src/main/java/cloud/samlo/couch/kevbox/update/SilentState.kt",
      "content": "`markRefused` always overwrites the stored code. If a late `STATUS_PENDING_USER_ACTION` result comes in for an older versionCode X after the state has already moved to a newer Y, this saves `(X, 0, refused)`. That wipes Y's attempt count, and the next run sees Y as fresh with 3 attempts again. The same applies to `recordAttempt`. Only write when the stored code matches, or when the incoming code is newer.",
      "suggestion_code": "    fun markRefused(context: Context, versionCode: Long) = synchronized(this) {\n        val stored = Prefs.of(context).getLong(CODE, -1L)\n        if (stored \u003e versionCode) return@synchronized\n        save(context, versionCode, snapshot(context, versionCode).attempts, true)\n    }",
      "existing_code": "    fun markRefused(context: Context, versionCode: Long) = synchronized(this) {\n        save(context, versionCode, snapshot(context, versionCode).attempts, true)\n    }",
      "start_line": 33,
      "end_line": 35,
      "category": "bug",
      "severity": "low"
    }
